@node Characters
@section Characters

Characters are objects that represent printed characters such as letters
and digits.  All Scheme implementations must support at least the
@acronym{ASCII} char@value{shy}ac@value{shy}ter repertoire: that is,
Unicode characters U+0000 through U+007F.
Im@value{shy}ple@value{shy}men@value{shy}tations may support any other
Unicode characters they see fit, and may also support non-Unicode
characters as well.  Except as otherwise specified, the result of
applying any of the following procedures to a non-Unicode character is
implementation-dependent.

Characters are written using the notation @code{#\}@angle{character} or
@code{#\}@angle{character name} or @code{#\x}@angle{hex scalar value}.

The following character names must be supported by all implementations
with the given values.  Implementations may add other names provided
they cannot be interpreted as hex scalar values preceded by @code{x}.

@indentedblock
@ifnothtml
@multitable {xxbackspace} {; the preferred way to write a space}
@item @ht{#\alarm} @tab ; U+0007
@item @ht{#\backspace} @tab ; U+0008
@item @ht{#\delete} @tab ; U+007F
@item @ht{#\escape} @tab ; U+001B
@item @ht{#\newline} @tab ; the linefeed character, U+000A
@item @ht{#\null} @tab ; the null character, U+0000
@item @ht{#\return} @tab ; the return character, U+000D
@item @ht{#\space} @tab ; the preferred way to write a space
@item @ht{#\tab} @tab ; the tab character, U+0009
@end multitable
@end ifnothtml
@ifhtml
@multitable @columnfractions .2 .8
@item @ht{#\alarm} @tab ; U+0007
@item @ht{#\backspace} @tab ; U+0008
@item @ht{#\delete} @tab ; U+007F
@item @ht{#\escape} @tab ; U+001B
@item @ht{#\newline} @tab ; the linefeed character, U+000A
@item @ht{#\null} @tab ; the null character, U+0000
@item @ht{#\return} @tab ; the return character, U+000D
@item @ht{#\space} @tab ; the preferred way to write a space
@item @ht{#\tab} @tab ; the tab character, U+0009
@end multitable
@end ifhtml
@end indentedblock

Here are some additional examples:

@indentedblock
@ifnothtml
@multitable {xxx3bb} {xi (if character and name are supported)x}
@item @ht{#\a} @tab ; lower case letter
@item @ht{#\A} @tab ; upper case letter
@item @ht{#\@U{0028}} @tab ; left parenthesis
@item @ht{#\ } @tab ; the space character
@item @ht{#\x03BB} @tab ; @U{03BB} (if character is supported)
@item @ht{@hashchar{}@backslashchar{}}@t{@charbody{iota}}
@tab ; @U{03B9} (if character and name are supported)
@end multitable
@end ifnothtml
@ifhtml
@multitable @columnfractions .15 .85
@item @ht{#\a} @tab ; lower case letter
@item @ht{#\A} @tab ; upper case letter
@item @ht{#\@U{0028}} @tab ; left parenthesis
@item @ht{#\ } @tab ; the space character
@item @ht{#\x03BB} @tab ; @U{03BB} (if character is supported)
@item @ht{@hashchar{}@backslashchar{}}@t{@charbody{iota}}
@tab ; @U{03B9} (if character and name are supported)
@end multitable
@end ifhtml
@end indentedblock

Case is significant in @code{#@backslashchar{}}@angle{character}, and in
@code{#@backslashchar{}}@angle{character name}, but not in
@code{#@backslashchar{}x}@angle{hex scalar value}.  If @angle{character}
in @code{#@backslashchar{}}@angle{character} is alphabetic, then any
character immediately following @angle{character} cannot be one that can
appear in an identifier.  This rule resolves the ambiguous case where,
for example, the sequence of characters @samp{@value{shy}space} could be
taken to be either a rep@value{shy}resen@value{shy}tation of the space
character or a representation of the character @code{#@backslashchar{}s}
fol@value{shy}lowed by a representation of the symbol @code{pace}.

Characters written in the @code{#@backslashchar{}} notation are
self-evaluating.  That is, they do not have to be quoted in programs.

Some of the procedures that operate on characters ignore the difference
between upper case and lower case.  The procedures that ignore case have
@nobr{@samp{-ci}} (for @quotedbl{case insensitive}) embedded in their
names.

@deffn procedure char? obj

Returns @code{#t} if @var{obj} is a character, otherwise returns @code{#f}.
@end deffn

@deffn  procedure char=? @pari{char} @parii{char} @pariii{char} @defdots{}
@deffnx procedure char<? @pari{char} @parii{char} @pariii{char} @defdots{}
@deffnx procedure char>? @pari{char} @parii{char} @pariii{char} @defdots{}
@deffnx procedure char<=? @pari{char} @parii{char} @pariii{char} @defdots{}
@deffnx procedure char>=? @pari{char} @parii{char} @pariii{char} @defdots{}

These procedures return @code{#t} if the results of passing their
arguments to @code{char->integer} are respectively equal,
mono@value{shy}ton@value{shy}ically in@value{shy}creasing,
mono@value{shy}ton@value{shy}ically de@value{shy}creasing,
mono@value{shy}ton@value{shy}ically non-decreasing, or
mono@value{shy}ton@value{shy}ically non-increasing.

These predicates are required to be transitive.
@end deffn

@deffn  {char library procedure} char-ci=? @
@pari{char} @parii{char} @pariii{char} @defdots{}
@deffnx {char library procedure} char-ci< @
@pari{char} @parii{char} @pariii{char} @defdots{}
@deffnx {char library procedure} char-ci>? @
@pari{char} @parii{char} @pariii{char} @defdots{}
@deffnx {char library procedure} char-ci<=? @
@pari{char} @parii{char} @pariii{char} @defdots{}
@deffnx {char library procedure} char-ci>=? @
@pari{char} @parii{char} @pariii{char} @defdots{}

These procedures are similar to @code{char=?} et cetera, but they treat
upper case and lower case letters as the same.  For example,
@code{(char-ci=? #@backslashchar{}A #@backslashchar{}a)}
re@value{shy}turns @code{#t}.

Specifically, these procedures behave as if @code{char-foldcase} were
applied to their arguments before they were compared.
@end deffn

@deffn  {char library procedure} char-alphabetic? char
@deffnx {char library procedure} char-numeric? char
@deffnx {char library procedure} char-whitespace? char
@deffnx {char library procedure} char-upper-case? letter
@deffnx {char library procedure} char-lower-case? letter

These procedures return @code{#t} if their arguments are alphabetic,
numeric, whitespace, upper case, or lower case characters, respectively,
otherwise they return @code{#f}.

Specifically, they must return @code{#t} when applied to characters with
the Unicode properties Alphabetic, Numeric_Type=Decimal, White_Space,
Uppercase, and Lowercase respectively, and @code{#f} when applied to any
other Unicode characters.  Note that many Unicode characters are
alphabetic but neither upper nor lower case.
@end deffn

@deffn {char library procedure} digit-value char

This procedure returns the numeric value (0 to 9) of its argument if it
is a numeric digit (that is, if @code{char-numeric?} returns @code{#t}),
or @code{#f} on any other char@value{shy}acter.

@lisp
(digit-value #\3)     @ok{3}
(digit-value #\x0664) @ok{4}
(digit-value #\x0AE6) @ok{0}
(digit-value #\x0EA6) @ok{#f}
@end lisp
@end deffn

@deffn  procedure char->integer char
@deffnx procedure integer->char n

Given a Unicode character, @code{char->integer} returns an exact integer
be@value{shy}tween 0 and @hc{#xD7FF} or between @hc{#xE000} and
@hc{#x10FFFF} which is equal to the Unicode scalar value of that
character.  Given a non-Unicode character, it re@value{shy}turns an
exact integer greater than @hc{#x10FFFF}.  This is true independent of
whether the implementation uses the Unicode representation internally.

Given an exact integer that is the value returned by a character when
@nobr{@code{char->integer}} is applied to it,
@nobr{@code{integer->char}} returns that character.
@end deffn

@deffn  {char library procedure} char-upcase char
@deffnx {char library procedure} char-downcase char
@deffnx {char library procedure} char-foldcase char

The @code{char-upcase} procedure, given an argument that is the
lowercase part of a Unicode casing pair, returns the uppercase member of
the pair, pro@value{shy}vided that both characters are
sup@value{shy}ported by the Scheme implementation.  Note that
language-sensitive casing pairs are not used.  If the argument is not
the lowercase member of such a pair, it is returned.

The @code{char-downcase} procedure, given an argument that is the
uppercase part of a Unicode casing pair, returns the lowercase member of
the pair, pro@value{shy}vided that both characters are
sup@value{shy}ported by the Scheme implemen@value{shy}ta@value{shy}tion.
Note that language-sensitive casing pairs are not used.  If the argument
is not the uppercase member of such a pair, it is returned.

The @code{char-foldcase} procedure applies the Unicode simple
case-folding al@value{shy}go@value{shy}rithm to its argument and returns
the result.  Note that language-sensitive folding is not used.  If the
character that results from folding is not sup@value{shy}ported by the
implementation, the argument is returned.  See @acronym{UAX}
@hashchar{}44@footnote{@value{refUnicodeAnnex44}} (part of the Unicode
Standard) for details.

Note that many Unicode lowercase characters do not have uppercase
equiv@value{shy}alents.
@end deffn
